---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Equation from "../../components/Equation.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Why Token Efficiency Still Matters — AI Research"
  description="Why token efficiency remains a systems constraint even as models, quantization, and inference hardware improve."
>
  <article>
    <div class="meta">AI Systems · Inference · Efficiency</div>
    <h1>Why Token Efficiency Still Matters</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Better hardware and cleverer model compression do not make tokens free.
      Every unnecessary token still consumes bandwidth, expands context, and
      adds cost to every step of an AI system.
    </p>

    <p>
      Token efficiency still matters because large language models are
      fundamentally constrained by memory bandwidth during inference and by the
      way attention cost grows with sequence length. Even breakthrough
      quantization methods such as <code>LLM.int8()</code> can only address part
      of the problem. Compressing weights reduces the cost of moving parameters
      into computation, but inefficient tokenization still taxes every forward
      pass. It inflates the <strong>KV cache</strong>, lengthens prompts and
      completions, and drives up operational cost. Raw model optimization
      cannot completely fix a sloppy data representation.
    </p>

    <h2>A token is not one uniform unit of cost</h2>
    <p>
      The word “token” hides several different bills. During training, more
      tokens mean more examples flowing through the model. During inference,
      prompt tokens are processed together in a prefill phase, while generated
      tokens arrive one at a time. Both phases use attention, but their work
      grows differently. The KV cache adds a separate memory cost that grows
      with the context kept alive.
    </p>

    <h2>Training: parameters meet data</h2>
    <p>
      A common first-order estimate for training a dense transformer is:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`C_{\mathrm{train}} \approx 6ND`}
      label="Training compute is approximately six times the number of parameters times the number of training tokens"
    />
    <p>
      Here, <strong>C<sub>train</sub></strong> is training computation in
      floating-point operations, <strong>N</strong> is the number of model
      parameters, and <strong>D</strong> is the number of training tokens. The
      factor of six is a rough accounting rule for the forward and backward
      matrix operations in a dense model. It is not a universal constant: the
      exact count depends on architecture and what operations are included.
      In particular, this compact estimate does not separately model the
      sequence-length cost of attention.
    </p>
    <p>
      The useful intuition is narrower than “every token always costs the
      same.” If the model and training recipe stay fixed, processing more
      training tokens generally increases the training work roughly in
      proportion to D. But this equation says nothing by itself about whether
      those extra tokens improve the model enough to justify their cost.
    </p>

    <h2>Inference has two phases</h2>
    <h3>1. Prefill: processing the prompt</h3>
    <p>
      Before the first answer token can be generated, the model processes the
      prompt of length P. For ordinary dense self-attention, the attention
      portion of that work scales approximately as:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`W_{\mathrm{prefill,attn}} = O(LP^2d)`}
      label="Prefill attention work grows in proportion to layers times prompt length squared times hidden width"
    />
    <p>
      <strong>L</strong> is the number of transformer layers, <strong>P</strong>
      is prompt length in tokens, and <strong>d</strong> is the hidden width.
      The square appears because attention compares tokens with other tokens in
      the prompt. Double P and this part of the work can approach four times as
      much. This is an asymptotic description of attention, not a latency
      prediction for a particular GPU; projections, MLPs, kernels, and memory
      traffic also matter.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Decode: producing the answer</h3>
    <p>
      Generation is sequential: the next token depends on the previous ones.
      At decode step j, attention reads the prompt and the j−1 answer tokens
      already produced. Across G generated tokens, the attention work is
      approximately:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`W_{\mathrm{decode,attn}} = O\!\left(Ld\left[GP + \frac{G(G-1)}{2}\right]\right)`}
      label="Decode attention work grows with layers and hidden width times generated tokens multiplied by prompt length plus the triangular sum of earlier generated tokens"
    />
    <p>
      <strong>G</strong> is the number of generated tokens. The term GP counts
      each generated token attending over the original prompt. The sum
      G(G−1)/2 counts generated tokens attending over earlier generated
      tokens: the first has none, the next has one, then two, and so on. This
      equation describes attention only. Each decode step also performs
      projections and feed-forward computation, and real latency depends on
      batching and hardware utilization.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why prompt tokens and generated tokens should be reported
      separately. A longer prompt affects prefill and is revisited by every
      generated step. A longer answer adds sequential steps and makes later
      attention steps read a longer history.
    </p>

    <h2>The memory bill: the KV cache</h2>
    <p>
      To avoid recomputing old keys and values at every decode step, a serving
      system keeps them in a key-value cache. A useful estimate for its size is:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`M_{\mathrm{KV}} \approx 2LBTn_{\mathrm{kv}}d_hb`}
      label="KV cache memory is approximately two times layers, batch size, context length, KV heads, head dimension, and bytes per value"
    />
    <p>
      <strong>M<sub>KV</sub></strong> is cache memory in bytes. The factor two
      accounts for keys and values. <strong>L</strong> is the number of layers,
      <strong>B</strong> the number of sequences served together,
      <strong>T</strong> the cached context length, <strong>n<sub>kv</sub></strong>
      the number of key/value heads, <strong>d<sub>h</sub></strong> each head’s
      width, and <strong>b</strong> the bytes used for each stored value.
    </p>
    <p>
      For an illustrative model with 32 layers, 8 KV heads, head width 128,
      and two-byte cache values, one sequence needs about 128 KiB per cached
      token. At 8,192 tokens that is about 1 GiB, before accounting for model
      weights, temporary buffers, or serving-system overhead. The estimate
      changes with the architecture, batch size, cache precision, and whether
      heads or cache blocks are shared. Its point is to show why context length
      can limit how many concurrent requests fit in memory.
    </p>

    <h2>Why quantization will not save you</h2>
    <p>
      It is tempting to think that clever engineering can simply squeeze the
      model down until token count stops mattering. <code>LLM.int8()</code> is a
      useful example of both what quantization can accomplish and where its
      boundary lies. The method isolates large emergent outlier features in
      higher-precision matrix operations while running the overwhelming
      majority of the remaining multiplications in 8-bit arithmetic. In the
      right setting, this substantially reduces the memory bandwidth required
      to load and process weights.
    </p>

    <p>
      But weight compression does not reduce the sheer volume of tokens moving
      through the system. If your vocabulary or serialization choices produce
      unnecessarily long sequences, you still have to embed, attend over,
      cache, and decode all of that context. The weight bottleneck may shrink
      while the activation and context bottlenecks remain. Quantization makes
      the pipeline more efficient; it does not make an inefficient workload
      disappear.
    </p>

    <h2>A different efficiency budget</h2>
    <p>
      Token count is only one part of inference cost. Precision routing asks a
      different question: how should values be represented and moved through
      memory? I am exploring whether activation statistics can guide that
      allocation under a fast-memory budget. It is a separate, untested
      hypothesis, described in
      <a href="/writing/precision-on-a-budget">Precision on a Budget</a>.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      Engineering a smaller pipeline is useless if you keep pouring twice as
      much work through it.
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Token economy is a product constraint</h2>
    <p>
      This is not only a low-level hardware concern. Token count affects
      latency, throughput, memory allocation, API cost, and the number of users
      a system can serve on a fixed fleet. It also shapes product behavior:
      longer prompts are harder to inspect, longer traces are harder to debug,
      and longer reasoning paths create more opportunities for an agent to
      drift or fail.
    </p>

    <p>
      The right response is not to optimize token count in isolation. A short
      sequence that loses the information needed for reliable reasoning is not
      efficient; it is merely incomplete. The goal is a better ratio between
      useful information and representation cost: compact schemas, deliberate
      retrieval, concise tool outputs, productive reasoning steps, and
      tokenizers that reflect the data they encode.
    </p>

    <h2>Efficiency means meeting a quality target</h2>
    <p>
      This gives us a more useful way to state the goal. Let π represent a
      system choice, such as how much context to retrieve, how compactly to
      encode it, or how long to let generation continue:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\min_{\pi}\; \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{serving\ cost}(\pi)] \quad \text{subject to} \quad Q(\pi) \ge Q_{\min}`}
      label="Choose a system policy that minimizes expected serving cost while keeping task quality above a minimum target"
    />
    <p>
      In plain language: reduce the expected cost of serving a request, but
      only among choices that still meet a minimum quality target. The policy
      π might change context selection, representation, or generation length.
      Q(π) is a task-appropriate quality measure, and Q<sub>min</sub> is the
      lowest acceptable score. Cost could mean compute, latency, memory, money,
      or a weighted combination; an experiment must say which one it measures.
      This also makes clear why “fewer tokens” is not automatically “better.”
    </p>

    <p>
      Building tight feedback loops around these choices is what separates a
      fragile prototype from an architecture that can handle millions of active
      users. Measure tokens alongside quality, latency, memory, and cost. Then
      optimize the whole workload rather than assuming that a smaller weight
      matrix solves every systems problem.
    </p>

